<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chp-looping-flow" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Looping Flow</title>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			In the previous chapter, <pf>if</pf>-statements gave your code the ability to 
			choose between different paths based on conditions. Now we introduce a different 
			kind of flow control: <term>repetition</term>. Loops let you repeat a block of 
			code multiple times, either a known number of times or until some condition 
			changes. Think of replacing dozens of copy-pasted lines with just a few 
			representative lines.
		</p>

		<p>
			MATLAB provides two primary looping structures:
			<dl width="narrow">
				<li><title>for-loops</title>
					<p>
						Repeat once for each value in a known list or range. Use these when you know 
						how many iterations you need before the loop starts.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li><title>while-loops</title>
					<p>
						Repeat as long as a condition remains true. Use these when the number of 
						iterations depends on something that changes during execution.
					</p>
				</li>
			</dl>
		</p>

		<p>
			The chapter also introduces three commands: <pf>continue</pf>, <pf>break</pf>, 
			and <pf>return</pf>, which let you skip iterations, exit loops early, or exit 
			functions immediately when the answer is already known.
		</p>

		<p>
			By the end of this chapter, you will be able to automate repetitive calculations, 
			accumulate running totals and products, search for values that satisfy conditions, 
			and approximate mathematical quantities to any desired accuracy.
		</p>

	</introduction>

	<xi:include href="sec-for-loop.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="sec-while-loop.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="sec-early-stopping-control.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="exercises-looping-flow.ptx" />

	<conclusion>
		<title>🗝️ Key Takeaways...</title>
		<assemblage xml:id="takeaways-looping-flow">
			<p>
				<dl width="narrow">
					<li>
						<title>For-Loops</title>
						<p>
							Repeats code for each value in a 
							<ul marker="square"	>
								<li>range ↔ <pf>1:n</pf></li>
								<li>range with step ↔ <pf>start:step:stop</pf></li>
								<li>list ↔ <pf>[1, 3, 5]</pf></li>
							</ul>
						</p>
						<p>MATLAB handles the loop variable ↔ you do not need to initialize or update it yourself.</p>
						<p>Is ideal when the number of iterations is known in advance</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<title>While-Loops</title>
						<p>Repeats code as long as a condition is true.</p>
						<p>You must initialize and update the variables yourself.</p>
						<p>Is ideal when the number of iterations is not known.</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<title>Common Loop Patterns</title>
						<p>Accumulation (running sums and products)</p>
						<p>Counting values that meet a condition</p>
						<p>Searching for the first value that satisfies a test</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<title>Loop Control Commands</title>
						<p>
							The commands
							<ul marker="square"	>
								<li><pf>continue</pf> ↔ skip the rest of the current iteration,</li>
								<li><pf>break</pf> ↔ exit the loop, and</li>
								<li><pf>return</pf> ↔ exit the function</li>
							</ul>
							modify standard code/loop flow.
						</p>
					</li>
				</dl>
			</p>
		</assemblage>
	</conclusion>

</chapter>